<!-- Author: Chrissy Safranski  -->

<setup>
  <implies name="twominsneg1maxpos">
    <boolean>$f.numMinima=2</boolean>
    <and>
      <boolean>$f.minimumValues[1]< 0</boolean>
      <boolean>$f.minimumValues[2]< 0</boolean>
      <boolean>$f.maximumValues[1]> 0</boolean>
    </and>
  </implies>
  <implies name="twomaxpos1minneg">
    <boolean>$f.numMaxima=2</boolean>
    <and>
      <boolean>$f.maximumValues[1]> 0</boolean>
      <boolean>$f.maximumValues[2]> 0</boolean>
      <boolean>$f.minimumValues[1]< 0</boolean>
    </and>
  </implies>

  <repeat name="allextremapos" for="$f.extremumValues" valueName="xi"><boolean>$xi> 0</boolean></repeat>
  <repeat name="allextremaneg" for="$f.extremumValues" valueName="xi"><boolean>$xi< 0</boolean></repeat>

  <!-- For no roots in a degree 4 poly, we need that $a4> 0 and all extrema are positive or $a4< 0 and all extrema are negative.  -->
    <and name="a4posallextremapos">
      <boolean>$a4> 0</boolean>
      $allextremapos
    </and>
    <and name="a4negallextremaneg">
      <boolean>$a4< 0</boolean>
      $allextremaneg
    </and>

  <!-- For 1 roots in a degree 4 poly, we need that numExtrema=1 and it looks like it barely touches the x-axis.  -->
  <and name="onezero">
    <boolean>$f.numExtrema=1</boolean>
    <boolean>abs($f.extremumValues)< .15</boolean>
  </and>

  <!-- For 2 roots in a degree 4 poly, we need that $a4< 0 and all extrema are positive or $a4> 0 and all extrema are negative.  -->
    <and name="a4negallextremapos">
      <boolean>$a4< 0</boolean>
      $allextremapos
    </and>
    <and name="a4posallextremaneg">
      <boolean>$a4> 0</boolean>
      $allextremaneg
    </and>
</setup>

Below you'll find a degree <m>4</m> polynomial of the form <me>p(x) = a_0 + a_1x + a_2x^2 + a_3x^3 + a_4x^4</me> where <m>a_0, \ldots, a_4</m> are set up as sliders.  In the questions that follow, you'll experiment with different values of <m>a_0, \ldots, a_4</m> to investigate different possible behaviors in a degree <m>4</m> polynomial.  Note that <m>a_4 \ne 0</m> is required for <m>p</m> to be a degree <m>4</m> polynomial.
<aside><title>Note on graph navigation</title>
You can swipe and drag to move around and explore the graph.  Also, the "-" and "+" buttons in the bottom right of the graph will zoom in and out, and the "O" button will return to the starting window.</aside>

<sideBySide widths="40% 57%">
  <div>
    <slider name="a4" from="-10" to="10" width="200" initialValue="1" step=".1"><label><m>a_4</m></label></slider>
    <slider name="a3" from="-10" to="10" width="200" initialValue="3" step=".1"><label><m>a_3</m></label></slider>
    <slider name="a2" from="-10" to="10" width="200" initialValue="1" step=".1"><label><m>a_2</m></label></slider>
    <slider name="a1" from="-10" to="10" width="200" initialValue="1" step=".1"><label><m>a_1</m></label></slider>
    <slider name="a0" from="-10" to="10" width="200" initialValue="0" step=".1"><label><m>a_0</m></label></slider>
  </div>
  <graph>
    <shortDescription>graph of a degree 4 polynomial whose coefficients are controlled by sliders</shortDescription>
    <function name="f" hide="$a4=0">$a4 x^4 + $a3 x^3 + $a2 x^2 + $a1 x + $a0</function>
    <label fixed anchor="(-5,6)" hide="$a4!=0">Make <m>a_4\neq 0</m>.</label>
    <label fixed anchor="(5.2,5)" hide="$a4!=0">When <m>a_4= 0</m>, the function <m>p(x)</m> is degree <m>3</m>, not <m>4</m>.</label>
    <function styleNumber="2" lineWidth="4" hide="!$showa4x4">$a4 x^4</function>
  </graph>
</sideBySide>

<cascade revealAll>
  <aside><title>a. Zeros of a degree <m>4</m> polynomial</title>
    <p>
      Hit Check Work when you have adjusted the sliders so that the graph crosses the <m>x</m>-axis <m>0</m> times. <answer disableAfterCorrect><award><when>$a4posallextremapos or $a4negallextremaneg</when></award></answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      Hit Check Work when you have adjusted the sliders so that the graph crosses the <m>x</m>-axis exactly <m>1</m> time. <answer disableAfterCorrect><award><when>$onezero</when></award></answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      Hit Check Work when you have adjusted the sliders so that the graph crosses the <m>x</m>-axis exactly <m>2</m> times. <answer disableAfterCorrect><award><when>$a4negallextremapos or $a4posallextremaneg</when></award></answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      Recall from the definition of a polynoimal function what we mean by a <term>zero</term> of the polynomial.
      <answer name="maxnumzeros"><label>What is the largest number zeros a degree <m>4</m> polynomial can have?</label>4</answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      Hit Check Work when you have adjusted the sliders so that the graph shows <conditionalContent><case condition="$maxnumzeros.creditAchieved=1"><m>4</m></case><else>this maximum number of</else></conditionalContent> zeros. <answer disableAfterCorrect><award><when>$f.numExtrema=3 and <and>$twomaxpos1minneg $twominsneg1maxpos</and></when></award></answer>
    </p>
    <p hide="$maxnumzeros.creditAchieved!=1">
      It is also possible to adjust the sliders so that the graph has exactly <m>3</m> zeros, but it's a little tricky.  Can you do it?
    </p>
  </aside>
  <aside><title>b. Turning points</title>
    <p>
      We say that a function has a <term>turning point</term> if the function changes from decreasing to increasing or increasing to decreasing at the point.  For example, any quadratic function has a turning point at its vertex.
	</p>
    <p>
      Recall that <m>p(x)</m> is the function shown in the graph.
      <answer name="maxturningpts"><label>What is the largest number of turning points that <m>p(x)</m> can have?</label>3</answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      Experiment with the sliders, and hit Check Work when <m>p(x)</m> has <conditionalContent><case condition="$maxturningpts.creditAchieved=1"><m>3</m></case><else>this maximum number of</else></conditionalContent> turning points. <answer disableAfterCorrect><award><when>$f.numExtrema=3</when></award></answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      Experiment with the sliders, and hit Check Work when <m>p(x)</m> has <conditionalContent><case condition="$maxturningpts.creditAchieved=1"><m>1</m> turning point.</case><else>a smaller number of turning points.</else></conditionalContent> <answer disableAfterCorrect><award><when>$f.numExtrema=1</when></award></answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      What other numbers of turning points are possible for <m>p(x)</m>?Experiment with the sliders and make a conjecture.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <aside><title>c. Long-range behavior</title>
    <p>
      What long-range behavior is possible for <m>p(x)</m>?
    </p>
    <p>
      Experiment with the sliders, and hit Check Work when <br/><m>p(x)\to \infty</m> as both <m>x\to \infty</m> and <m>x\to -\infty</m>. <answer disableAfterCorrect><award><when>$a4> 0 </when></award></answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      Experiment with the sliders, and hit Check Work when <br/><m>p(x)\to -\infty</m> as both <m>x\to \infty</m> and <m>x\to -\infty</m>. <answer disableAfterCorrect><award><when>$a4< 0 </when></award></answer>
    </p>
    <!-- <p>
      Is it possible for the long-range behavior of <m>p(x)</m> to be different as <m>x\to \infty</m> compared to as <m>p(x)\to -\infty</m>?  Said differently, do you think it's possible that <me>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} p(x) \neq \displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} p(x)?</me>
    </p> -->
    <p>
      Click on the box below to plot <m>y = a_4 x^4</m> on the graph, and compare <m>p(x)</m> and <m>a_4 x^4</m>. How do they look when we zoom out? (Experiment with different values of each of the sliders, too.) <br/>
      <booleanInput name="showa4x4"><label>Show <m>a_4 x^4</m> on graph</label></booleanInput>
    </p>
    <div sectionWideCheckWork colorAnswersSeparately>
    <p>
      <answer><choiceInput inline><label>The functions <m>p(x)</m> and <m>a_4 x^4</m> </label><choice credit="1">look similar</choice><choice>look different</choice></choiceInput></answer> as we zoom out and <answer><choiceInput inline><label><m>x</m> gets </label><choice>smaller and smaller</choice><choice credit="1">larger and larger</choice></choiceInput></answer> in either direction, although <answer><choiceInput inline><label>they can </label><choice>look similar</choice><choice credit="1">look different</choice></choiceInput></answer> in the middle. <answer><choiceInput inline><label>Since <m>x^4</m> is</label><choice credit="1">always positive</choice><choice>always negative</choice><choice>sometimes positive, sometimes negative</choice></choiceInput></answer> regardless of whether <m>x</m> is positive or negative, <answer><choiceInput inline><label>this means that <m>p(x)</m></label><choice>has different behavior</choice><choice credit="1">has the same behavior</choice></choiceInput></answer> as <m>x\to\infty</m> as compared to when <m>x\to -\infty</m>.</p>
    <p>
      It also means that the long-range behavior of <m>p(x)</m> depends only on the sign of <m>a_4</m>, and that <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>behavior of limits</shortDescription><choice><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} p(x) \neq \displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} p(x)</m></choice><choice credit="1"><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} p(x) = \displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} p(x)</m></choice></choiceInput></answer>.
    </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      Can you see how this behavior affects the possible number of turning points from part b.?
    </p>
  </aside>
</cascade>